int a = b; — Is it an AssignExpr?
No. int a = b; is a GlobalVarDecl with:

vAST.T = IntType
vAST.I = Identifier "a"
vAST.E = VarExpr(SimpleVar("b")) ← just the initializer expression
AssignExpr only appears for a standalone assignment statement like a = b; inside a function body. A declaration with initializer is not an AssignExpr — the = in a declaration is syntax sugar, not a runtime assignment expression.

VarExpr and SimpleVar Relationship
VarExpr wraps a Variable (which can be SimpleVar or an array-indexed var). For b in int a = b;:

VarExpr
  └─ V: SimpleVar
           └─ I: Identifier("b")
                   └─ decl → points to GlobalVarDecl for b (set by type checker)
So visitVarExpr just delegates:

ast.V.visit(this, o);  // goes to visitSimpleVar
Nothing else. VarExpr does zero work itself — it's a wrapper.

visitSimpleVar does the actual work: looks at ast.I.decl to find the declaration, then emits the correct load instruction.

The Key Principle You're Missing: Operators Are Not Values
The JVM operand stack only ever holds values — never operators. Operators are encoded in the instruction itself, not pushed onto the stack. So:

iadd is an instruction that pops two ints and pushes one int
There is no "push the + operator onto the stack" step
The operator literally doesn't appear on the stack at any point
Frame Responsibility at Each Layer
The rule is: every expression visitor leaves exactly 1 net item on the stack and is fully responsible for its own frame tracking.

SimpleVar — emits a load instruction, pushes 1:

emit(iload_2)    → JVM: slot 2 value appears on stack
frame.push()     → tracks it
net: +1
VarExpr — just delegates, net: +1 (SimpleVar handled it)

UnaryExpr -x:

visit E          → child does frame.push(), cur+1, stack: [x]
emit ineg        → JVM: pops x, pushes -x, stack: [-x]
frame.pop()      → tracks x consumed
frame.push()     → tracks -x produced
net: +1
The -x replaces x — depth doesn't change from the unary op itself, but the total net from before visiting is +1.

BinaryExpr a + b:

visit E1         → child does frame.push(), cur+1, stack: [..., a]
visit E2         → child does frame.push(), cur+1, stack: [..., a, b]
emit iadd        → JVM: pops a and b, pushes a+b, stack: [..., a+b]
frame.pop(2)     → tracks both consumed
frame.push()     → tracks result produced
net: +1
So every expression visitor — no matter how complex — leaves exactly +1 on the stack. The caller never needs to add frame.push() after visiting a child expression — the child always handles it.

AssignExpr — Why Partially Correct
AssignExpr (E1 = LHS, E2 = RHS) for a = 5 as a statement:

The complication: assignment in VC is an expression (it has a value). So c = a = 5 is legal. This means visitAssignExpr must leave the assigned value on the stack, even if the caller is a statement:

visit E2          → pushes the value, stack: [5]
dup               → stack: [5, 5]
store into E1     → pops one copy, stores to variable, stack: [5]
                  → one copy remains as the "return value" of assignment
net: +1
Then visitExprStmt pops that remaining value when assignment is used as a statement:

ast.E.visit(this, o);   // visitAssignExpr leaves 1 on stack
emit(JVM.POP);          // discard it, we're in a statement context
frame.pop();
Your intuition "apart from visit lower expr, nothing else" was wrong because AssignExpr has the extra responsibility of storing back to E1 and the dup to preserve the expression value